/* Lesson quizzes — batch 3 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p5m1":[
 {id:"p5m1q1", q:"Cold load of https://shop.example.com over HTTP/3 (QUIC). The RTT is 30 ms, DNS is not cached and costs one RTT, and the server needs 150 ms to build the page. What is the time to first byte?", options:["210 ms","240 ms","270 ms","180 ms"], answer:1, why:"DNS 30 + QUIC's combined transport-and-TLS setup 30 + request 30 + server 150 = 240 ms. 270 ms is the HTTP/2 figure, which pays separate TCP and TLS 1.3 round trips; QUIC folds them into one."},
 {id:"p5m1q2", q:"A new connection starts with an initial congestion window of 10 segments x 1,460 bytes (about 14.6 KB), doubling every round trip. How many round trips of data does a 40 KB HTML file need, and how many does the same file Brotli-compressed to 12 KB need?", options:["3 and 1","3 and 2","2 and 1","1 and 1 on any broadband line"], answer:2, why:"The flights carry 14.6 KB, then 29.2 KB more (43.8 KB in total), so 40 KB needs two round trips and 12 KB fits in the first. Three assumes the window never doubles; link bandwidth is irrelevant because slow start caps the early flights."},
 {id:"p5m1q3", q:"Two scripts sit in <head>: <script src=\"/js/lib.js\"></script> then <script src=\"/js/app.js\"></script>. app.js needs lib.js to have run, and its document.querySelector('#cart') returns null. Which change fixes the null while keeping lib.js first?", options:["Add async to both tags","Add async to app.js only","Add type=\"text/javascript\" to both tags","Add defer to both tags"], answer:3, why:"defer downloads in parallel and executes after parsing, in document order, so #cart exists and lib.js still runs first. async executes each file the moment it arrives, in any order, so app.js could run before lib.js or before #cart is parsed."},
 {id:"p5m1q4", q:"A slide-out menu animates its left property from -280px to 0 and stutters whenever a large script is running. Which rewrite should stay smooth even while the main thread is busy?", options:["Animate transform from translateX(-280px) to translateX(0)","Animate margin-left instead of left","Animate width from 0 to 280px","Keep animating left, but in smaller requestAnimationFrame steps"], answer:0, why:"transform on its own layer is handled by the compositor off the main thread, skipping layout and paint. margin-left and width are geometry changes that force layout, paint and composite on the busy main thread just as left does; smaller steps do not change that cost."},
 {id:"p5m1q5", q:"A client says the home page is slow. DevTools Network, Timing for the document request: DNS Lookup 3 ms, Initial connection 35 ms, SSL 18 ms, Waiting for server response 1,850 ms, Content Download 11 ms. What should you fix first?", options:["Compress the hero images and convert them to a modern format such as AVIF","Ask the client to upgrade their office internet connection","Speed up the back end, for example by caching the rendered page","Inline the CSS to remove the render-blocking stylesheet"], answer:2, why:"Waiting for server response is the server's time to produce the first byte; 1,850 ms there dwarfs every network bar, so caching or a faster back end is the fix. Images and CSS are fetched only after this document arrives, so optimizing them cannot shorten this wait."}
],
"p5m2":[
 {id:"p5m2q1", q:"Grey text has relative luminance 0.20 and sits on a white background (luminance 1.0). Under WCAG 2.2 AA, where may this colour pair be used?", options:["Body text and large text, since the ratio exceeds 4:1","Neither, since the ratio is below 4.5:1","Large text only, since the ratio is 4.2:1","Body text only, since large text needs a higher ratio"], answer:2, why:"(1.0 + 0.05) / (0.20 + 0.05) = 1.05 / 0.25 = 4.2:1. That fails the 4.5:1 body-text minimum but clears the 3:1 minimum for large text. 'Neither' forgets that large text has the lower 3:1 threshold."},
 {id:"p5m2q2", q:"The site header's logo is an <img> and it is the only content inside <a href=\"/\">. Which alt text is correct?", options:["alt=\"\" so screen readers skip the decorative logo","alt=\"Riverside Roofing home page\"","alt=\"Blue circular logo with a white roof outline\"","alt=\"logo.png\""], answer:1, why:"An image that is the only content of a link is functional: its alt becomes the link's accessible name, so it should say where the link goes. An empty alt leaves an empty link announced only as 'link'; a visual description says what it looks like, not what it does."},
 {id:"p5m2q3", q:"A screen reader announces a form's radio buttons as 'Phone, radio button, 1 of 3', never mentioning the question 'Preferred contact method' shown above them in a <p>. What is the right fix?", options:["Put aria-label=\"Preferred contact method\" on each radio button","Add placeholder=\"Preferred contact method\" to the first radio","Change the <p> into an <h6> so it is announced first","Wrap the radios in a <fieldset> whose <legend> is the question"], answer:3, why:"A <fieldset> with a <legend> groups the radios so the question is announced together with each option. aria-label on each radio would replace their names, so users would hear the question three times and lose the choices Phone, Email and Text."},
 {id:"p5m2q4", q:"After a failed submit, a script should move focus to an error summary <div id=\"errors\"> so screen-reader users hear the list of problems. The div must not become a normal Tab stop. What do you do?", options:["Add tabindex=\"-1\" to the div, then call errors.focus()","Add tabindex=\"1\" to the div so it is reached first","Add tabindex=\"0\" to the div, then call errors.focus()","Add aria-hidden=\"true\" to the div until errors exist"], answer:0, why:"tabindex=\"-1\" makes an element focusable from script without adding it to the Tab order, which is exactly the error-summary case. tabindex=\"0\" works but leaves a permanent Tab stop; a positive tabindex scrambles the page's whole focus order."},
 {id:"p5m2q5", q:"Which snippet breaks a rule of ARIA?", options:["<button aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"nav\">Menu</button>","<a href=\"/services\" aria-current=\"page\">Services</a>","<a href=\"/cart\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Cart (2)</a>","<p id=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">3 results found</p>"], answer:2, why:"aria-hidden=\"true\" removes the link from the accessibility tree, yet it stays focusable, so keyboard users land on something the screen reader cannot describe. The others are textbook: aria-expanded on a menu button, aria-current on the active link, aria-live on a status region."}
],
"p5m3":[
 {id:"p5m3q1", q:"A box has width: 250px; padding: 15px; border: 3px solid; margin: 10px. How wide is it from border edge to border edge under the default content-box, and how wide is its content area under border-box?", options:["286 px and 214 px","250 px and 214 px","306 px and 214 px","286 px and 250 px"], answer:0, why:"content-box adds padding and border to the set width: 250 + 2x15 + 2x3 = 286 px. border-box keeps the box at 250 px, so content shrinks to 250 - 30 - 6 = 214 px. Margins sit outside the border in both models, so 306 px is wrong."},
 {id:"p5m3q2", q:"The stylesheet declares @layer components, utilities; then: @layer components { #nav .btn { color: red; } } @layer utilities { .text-white { color: white; } } and, outside any layer, a { color: black; }. What color is <a class=\"btn text-white\"> inside #nav?", options:["Red, because #nav .btn has the highest specificity","White, because utilities is declared after components","Red, because an id beats any layer ordering","Black, because unlayered styles beat every layer"], answer:3, why:"The cascade compares layers before specificity, and unlayered author styles outrank all layered ones, so even the bare type selector a wins. Specificity only decides between rules in the same layer; that is why the id rule's (1,1,0) does not help it here."},
 {id:"p5m3q3", q:"The root font size is 16 px and every <ul> has font-size: 1.25em. What is the font size of text in a list nested three levels deep, and what would it be if the rule used 1.25rem instead?", options:["20 px and 20 px","25 px and 20 px","31.25 px and 20 px","31.25 px and 31.25 px"], answer:2, why:"em is relative to the parent's font size, so it compounds: 16 x 1.25 = 20, then 25, then 31.25 px at the third level. rem always refers to the root, so every level stays at 16 x 1.25 = 20 px."},
 {id:"p5m3q4", q:"With a 16 px root font size, what is font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.75rem + 1vw, 1.5rem) on a 600 px wide viewport and on a 1,600 px wide viewport?", options:["18 px and 28 px","18 px and 24 px","16 px and 24 px","16 px and 28 px"], answer:1, why:"0.75rem is 12 px and 1vw is 1% of the width. At 600 px: 12 + 6 = 18 px, inside the 16-24 px range. At 1,600 px: 12 + 16 = 28 px, which clamp caps at the 1.5rem maximum of 24 px. 28 px forgets the upper bound."},
 {id:"p5m3q5", q:"Your rule .price-tag { color: #c0392b; } is visible in site.css in the Sources panel, yet the Styles pane for the price element lists other site.css rules but not this one at all, not even struck through. What is the most likely cause?", options:["A more specific rule won the cascade; lower its specificity","The selector does not match; compare the element's class attribute","The color value is invalid; hover the warning icon next to it","color does not inherit, so it must be set on the parent element instead"], answer:1, why:"The Styles pane lists every rule whose selector matches, so a missing rule means the selector does not match, often a misspelled class like price_tag. A rule that lost the cascade would still be listed, struck through, and an invalid value shows a warning icon."}
],
"p5m4":[
 {id:"p5m4q1", q:"A 450 px flex row with gap: 10px holds two items containing only short text: A is flex: 0 1 400px and B is flex: 0 3 200px. How wide does each end up?", options:["A 360 px, B 80 px","A 336 px, B 104 px","A 320 px, B 120 px","A 340 px, B 110 px"], answer:1, why:"Overflow is 400 + 200 + 10 - 450 = 160 px, taken in proportion to flex-shrink x basis: 400 vs 600. A gives up 64 px (336), B gives up 96 px (104). Splitting 1:3 by flex-shrink alone ignores the bases; 340/110 forgets the gap."},
 {id:"p5m4q2", q:"A gallery uses grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(180px, 1fr)) with gap: 20px inside a 940 px container. How many columns appear, and how wide is each?", options:["4 columns of 220 px","5 columns of 188 px","4 columns of 235 px","5 columns of 172 px"], answer:0, why:"Columns = floor((940 + 20) / (180 + 20)) = floor(4.8) = 4, and each gets (940 - 3 x 20) / 4 = 220 px. Five columns would need 5 x 180 + 4 x 20 = 980 px; 235 px forgets to subtract the gaps."},
 {id:"p5m4q3", q:"An <img> has srcset=\"roof-400.jpg 400w, roof-800.jpg 800w, roof-1200.jpg 1200w, roof-1600.jpg 1600w\" and sizes=\"(min-width: 48em) 50vw, 100vw\". Which file does a 1,024 px wide tablet with a device-pixel ratio of 2 download?", options:["roof-400.jpg","roof-800.jpg","roof-1200.jpg","roof-1600.jpg"], answer:2, why:"1,024 px is wider than 48em (768 px), so the image fills 50vw = 512 CSS px, and at DPR 2 it needs 1,024 device pixels; 1200w is the smallest candidate that covers that. 800w ignores the pixel ratio; 1600w ignores the 50vw slot."},
 {id:"p5m4q4", q:"A header with position: sticky; top: 0 scrolls away with the page instead of sticking. Its parent wrapper has overflow: hidden, added years ago to contain floats. What is the fix?", options:["Give the header z-index: 1000 so it stays above the content","Change the header to position: absolute; top: 0 inside the wrapper","Add position: relative to the parent wrapper so sticky has a reference","Remove overflow: hidden from the wrapper, or use overflow: clip"], answer:3, why:"sticky works only within its scroll container, and overflow: hidden makes the wrapper that container, so the header sticks inside a box that scrolls away. overflow: clip clips without creating a scroll container. z-index changes stacking order, not whether an element sticks."},
 {id:"p5m4q5", q:"A dropdown inside the site header has position: absolute; z-index: 9999 but still renders beneath the hero below it. The header has transform: translateZ(0) and no z-index; the hero has position: relative; z-index: 1. What fixes it?", options:["Raise the dropdown to z-index: 99999 so nothing can cover it","Give the header position: relative and a z-index of 2","Change the dropdown to position: fixed so it leaves the header","Add isolation: isolate to the dropdown to give it its own layer"], answer:1, why:"The header's transform creates a stacking context at level 0, so its children compete only inside it and the hero's z-index: 1 paints above the whole header. Raising the header's own z-index above the hero fixes it; no z-index on the dropdown can escape its parent's context."}
],
"p5m5":[
 {id:"p5m5q1", q:"What does this log? const qty = \"3\", price = 4; console.log(qty + price, qty * price, qty - \"1\");", options:["7 12 2","34 12 2","34 NaN NaN","34 12 NaN"], answer:1, why:"+ concatenates when either side is a string, giving \"34\"; every other arithmetic operator converts its operands to numbers, so \"3\" * 4 is 12 and \"3\" - \"1\" is 2. The 7 answer assumes + converts to numbers like the others do."},
 {id:"p5m5q2", q:"What does this log? const fns = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i * 10); console.log(fns.map(f => f()));", options:["[0, 10, 20]","[20, 20, 20]","[30, 30, 30]","[undefined, undefined, undefined]"], answer:2, why:"var creates one function-scoped i shared by all three closures, and the loop leaves it at 3 before any closure runs, so each returns 30. With let, each iteration gets a fresh binding and the result would be [0, 10, 20]."},
 {id:"p5m5q3", q:"What does this log? const ages = [25, 100, 9, 31]; const sorted = ages.sort(); console.log(sorted, ages);", options:["[9, 25, 31, 100] [25, 100, 9, 31]","[100, 25, 31, 9] [25, 100, 9, 31]","[9, 25, 31, 100] [9, 25, 31, 100]","[100, 25, 31, 9] [100, 25, 31, 9]"], answer:3, why:"The default sort compares values as strings, so \"100\" < \"25\" < \"31\" < \"9\", and sort mutates the array in place and returns that same array. Numeric order needs sort((a, b) => a - b); toSorted() would leave ages unchanged."},
 {id:"p5m5q4", q:"What does this log? const job = { client: \"Ruiz\", site: { city: \"Austin\" } }; const copy = { ...job, client: \"Lee\" }; copy.site.city = \"Dallas\"; console.log(job.client, job.site.city);", options:["Ruiz Dallas","Ruiz Austin","Lee Dallas","Lee Austin"], answer:0, why:"Spread makes a shallow copy: client is a new top-level field on copy, but site still points to the same nested object, so changing copy.site.city changes job's too. structuredClone(job) would give an independent deep copy and leave Austin."},
 {id:"p5m5q5", q:"You open index.html by double-clicking it. It loads <script type=\"module\" src=\"app.js\">, and the Console says the module was blocked by CORS policy from origin 'null'. What fixes it?", options:["Serve the folder with a local web server and open it over http://localhost","Remove type=\"module\" from the tag and keep the import statements","Drop the .js extension from the import paths so they resolve","Add defer to the module script tag so it waits for the page"], answer:0, why:"Module scripts obey CORS, and a file:// page has origin null, so the browser refuses to load them; any local web server fixes it. Removing type=\"module\" just produces 'Cannot use import statement outside a module', and browsers require the .js extension."}
],
"p5m6":[
 {id:"p5m6q1", q:"What order are the letters logged? setTimeout(() => console.log('T'), 0); (async () => { console.log('1'); await null; console.log('2'); await null; console.log('3'); })(); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('P')); console.log('S');", options:["1 S 2 3 P T","S 1 2 3 P T","1 S P 2 3 T","1 S 2 P 3 T"], answer:3, why:"The async function runs synchronously to its first await (1), then S logs. Microtasks drain in order: the continuation logs 2 and its second await queues a new microtask behind P, so P logs, then 3. The timer task T runs last. Each await yields, so the function does not finish in one go."},
 {id:"p5m6q2", q:"A dashboard loads three independent APIs that take 250 ms, 400 ms and 150 ms. Roughly how long does it take awaiting them one after another, and how long with await Promise.all([...])?", options:["800 ms and 400 ms","800 ms and 800 ms","400 ms and 150 ms","800 ms and 267 ms"], answer:0, why:"Sequential awaits add up: 250 + 400 + 150 = 800 ms. Promise.all starts all three at once and resolves when the slowest finishes, about 400 ms. await pauses only this function, not the requests, so running them concurrently is your choice."},
 {id:"p5m6q3", q:"At startup the code runs document.querySelectorAll('.delete').forEach(b => b.addEventListener('click', onDelete)). Delete buttons on to-do items added later do nothing. What is the best fix?", options:["Add { passive: true } to each listener so clicks are not blocked","Call e.stopPropagation() inside onDelete so the click reaches it","Listen on the list and find the button with e.target.closest('.delete')","Add new items with list.innerHTML += so they pick up the listeners"], answer:2, why:"Listeners attach only to elements that exist at that moment. Clicks bubble, so one delegated listener on the parent handles current and future items. innerHTML += re-parses the list and destroys the listeners already attached, making things worse."},
 {id:"p5m6q4", q:"In search-as-you-type, typing \"gutter\" sometimes leaves results for \"gu\" on screen, although every request returns 200. What is happening and what fixes it?", options:["The browser cached the first answer; add a cache-busting query string","An older, slower response lands last; abort the previous request","fetch rejected silently; wrap the call in try/catch to surface it","The input event fires too late; listen for keydown instead"], answer:1, why:"Responses can return out of order: the broad \"gu\" query may take longer than \"gutter\" and overwrite it. Aborting the previous request on each input (plus a debounce) guarantees only the latest result renders. A try/catch handles failures, but nothing here failed."},
 {id:"p5m6q5", q:"A customer profile page renders each customer's website with a.textContent = site; a.setAttribute('href', site); where site is whatever the customer typed. What risk remains, and what is the fix?", options:["None, because textContent already escapes the value safely","XSS through the link text; set it with innerHTML and escaped quotes","Token theft; store the site value in localStorage instead","A javascript: URL runs when clicked; allow only https: links"], answer:3, why:"textContent makes the link text safe, but the href is interpreted as a URL, and a javascript: URL executes when clicked. Allowing only the https: protocol closes it. 'None' is the tempting answer because textContent alone looks like the whole rule."}
],
"p5m7":[
 {id:"p5m7q1", q:"A plumbing company wants an 8-page marketing site. You, not the owner, will change the content a few times a year; it must rank well in search and load fast on budget phones. Which approach fits best?", options:["A React single-page app that renders every page in the browser","Static pages built at deploy time, served from a static host and CDN","Server-side rendering on a VPS that you patch and scale","A WordPress install so the content lives in a database"], answer:1, why:"Rarely changing, developer-edited content suits static generation: rebuilding is cheap, hosting is nearly free, and plain HTML paints fast and indexes well. A client-rendered SPA ships an empty shell and heavy JS, hurting first paint on phones and SEO; a CMS only pays off when the owner edits."},
 {id:"p5m7q2", q:"Published versions of a package are 3.4.2, 3.4.7, 3.9.0 and 4.0.0. With no lockfile, what does a fresh npm install resolve for \"^3.4.2\", and what for \"~3.4.2\"?", options:["3.9.0 and 3.4.7","4.0.0 and 3.9.0","3.4.7 and 3.4.7","4.0.0 and 3.4.7"], answer:0, why:"npm picks the highest version that satisfies the range. A caret allows anything below the next major, so ^3.4.2 gets 3.9.0 but never 4.0.0; a tilde allows only patch updates, so ~3.4.2 gets 3.4.7. Committing package-lock.json and using npm ci pins the exact tree."},
 {id:"p5m7q3", q:"You are moving a client's site, currently on an old host, to a static host. Which DNS plan is correct?", options:["Put CNAME records on both the apex and www, then delete any old records you do not recognize","Raise the TTL to 86400 beforehand, then replace the whole zone with the host's records","Point www at the host's IP with an A record and delete the MX records, since email is unrelated to the website","Lower the TTL to 300 a day ahead; use A/AAAA or ALIAS for the apex and a CNAME for www; keep MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC as they are"], answer:3, why:"A low TTL lets you revert a mistake in minutes, a true CNAME is not allowed at the apex, and the mail records keep the business's email working. Deleting unfamiliar records is the classic way to take a client's email offline during a website migration."},
 {id:"p5m7q4", q:"PageSpeed Insights field data for a client's phones, 75th percentile: LCP 2.2 s, INP 410 ms, CLS 0.05. Lighthouse in your browser scores 96. What should you work on?", options:["Nothing, because a lab score of 96 means real users have a fast site","LCP: give the hero image fetchpriority=\"high\" and convert it to AVIF","INP: cut and split the long JavaScript tasks, including third-party tags","CLS: add width and height attributes to every image and embed"], answer:2, why:"LCP (2.2 s, good is 2.5 s or less) and CLS (0.05, good is 0.1 or less) already pass; INP at 410 ms exceeds the 200 ms good threshold. Field data from real phones is what Google ranks on; one lab load on a fast machine can look perfect while INP fails."},
 {id:"p5m7q5", q:"A client's site showed a certificate warning on a Saturday morning. Certbot's renewal timer had been failing silently since a server upgrade, and nobody was warned. What should your operations setup have included?", options:["Relying on the expiry-warning emails Let's Encrypt sends to the account address","A five-year commercial certificate so renewal comes up rarely","A lower DNS TTL so a replacement certificate spreads faster","Certificate-expiry monitoring that alerts your phone well before the date"], answer:3, why:"Let's Encrypt stopped sending expiry emails in June 2025, and certificates issued from March 2026 last at most 200 days, so long-lived certificates are not an option. Renewal must be automated and independently monitored; DNS TTL has nothing to do with certificate validity."}
],
"p5m8":[
 {id:"p5m8q1", q:"A proxy loses the connection after forwarding a request and must decide whether to resend it. Which request is safe to resend without risking a duplicated effect?", options:["POST /api/orders with a new order","PATCH /api/counter with {\"increment\": 1}","PUT /api/customers/42 with the full customer record","POST /api/payments without an Idempotency-Key header"], answer:2, why:"PUT replaces the resource at a known URL, so sending it twice leaves the same state as once: it is idempotent. POST creates or acts, so a resend can create a second order or charge, and an increment PATCH adds again. That is why payment APIs accept an Idempotency-Key."},
 {id:"p5m8q2", q:"Pick the status codes, in order, for: (1) well-formed JSON whose sqft field is \"big\"; (2) a request to /api/admin with no session cookie; (3) a logged-in customer requesting the admin list; (4) a client exceeding its rate limit.", options:["422, 401, 403, 429","400, 403, 401, 429","422, 403, 401, 503","400, 401, 403, 500"], answer:0, why:"Valid JSON with invalid fields is 422 (400 is for malformed syntax). No login is 401; a known user who is not allowed is 403. A per-client rate limit is 429 with Retry-After. Swapping 401 and 403 is the most common mistake."},
 {id:"p5m8q3", q:"Nginx returns 504 Gateway Timeout on /api/report after 60 seconds, while every other endpoint of the same Node app answers normally. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The Node app has crashed and needs restarting with systemctl","That handler is too slow, such as a missing index or a hung outgoing call","Nginx itself is broken and should be reinstalled or restarted","The browser sent a malformed request, so the front end must be fixed"], answer:1, why:"504 from a proxy means the app behind it answered too slowly; since other routes work, the app is up and this handler is slow, often a missing index or a hung outgoing call. A crashed app gives 502 on every route; a malformed request would get a 4xx."},
 {id:"p5m8q4", q:"Which Set-Cookie header makes a login cookie for https://portal.example.com unreadable by JavaScript, HTTPS-only, withheld from cross-site form POSTs, and impossible for a sibling subdomain to overwrite?", options:["Set-Cookie: sid=...; Domain=example.com; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=None","Set-Cookie: sid=...; Path=/; Secure; SameSite=Lax","Set-Cookie: __Host-sid=...; Domain=example.com; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict","Set-Cookie: __Host-sid=...; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"], answer:3, why:"HttpOnly hides it from scripts, Secure limits it to HTTPS, Lax withholds it from cross-site POSTs, and the __Host- prefix forbids Domain so subdomains cannot overwrite it. The __Host- version with a Domain and no Secure would simply be rejected by the browser."},
 {id:"p5m8q5", q:"Testing the lesson's Express API: curl -i -X POST localhost:3000/api/quotes -d '{\"name\":\"Ana Ruiz\",\"phone\":\"210 555 0147\",\"sqft\":2400}' returns 422 with errors on name, phone and sqft, though the JSON is valid. Why?", options:["sqft must be sent as a string, so 2400 should be quoted as \"2400\"","The phone pattern rejects spaces, so the number must be sent as digits only","curl -d labels the body as a form, so express.json() never parses it","Express needs app.use(express.urlencoded()) before it can read JSON bodies"], answer:2, why:"express.json() parses only requests labelled application/json; curl -d labels the body as a form, so req.body is undefined, the validator receives an empty object, and every field fails. Adding -H 'Content-Type: application/json' fixes it; the validator wants an integer sqft, and its phone pattern allows spaces."}
],
"p5m9":[
 {id:"p5m9q1", q:"The quote API validates name as 2-80 characters. The admin page builds rows with tr.innerHTML = '<td>' + q.name + '</td>', and a submitted name containing markup runs script in the admin's browser. Which fix addresses the root cause?", options:["Encode on output: build the cell with td.textContent, backed by a CSP","Tighten the validation pattern so names can never contain angle brackets","Store each name base64-encoded in the database and decode it on display","Use a parameterized query for the INSERT that saves the quote"], answer:0, why:"XSS is an output problem: the data became markup where it landed, so encoding for the HTML context (textContent) is the real defense, with CSP behind it. Stricter validation helps but valid data can still be dangerous in another context, and parameterized queries stop SQL injection, not XSS."},
 {id:"p5m9q2", q:"A list endpoint lets users pick sort=newest or sort=size and builds 'SELECT * FROM quotes ORDER BY ' + req.query.sort. What is the correct fix?", options:["Replace the concatenation with a ? placeholder for the column name","Escape any quote characters in req.query.sort before concatenating","Reject sort values longer than 20 characters or containing spaces","Map the choice through an allow-list of known column names"], answer:3, why:"Placeholders bind values, not identifiers, so a column name cannot be a ? parameter; mapping the user's choice to a fixed set of known column names means user text never reaches the SQL. Escaping quotes or limiting length still lets input shape the query."},
 {id:"p5m9q3", q:"A site's session cookie is SameSite=None; Secure; HttpOnly because of an old embed. A page on another site auto-submits a hidden form to /account/email and changes logged-in victims' addresses. Which defense set is recommended?", options:["SameSite=Lax, a CSRF token on each state-changing request, and an Origin check","A CORS policy that allows only https://www.example.com to call /account/email","Server-side validation that the new email address is well formed and unique","Switching the endpoint from POST to GET so cross-site forms cannot reach it"], answer:0, why:"CSRF works because the browser attaches cookies automatically; Lax withholds them from cross-site POSTs, the token proves the request came from your page, and the Origin check refuses foreign senders. CORS only governs reading responses, so the forged form is still sent and processed."},
 {id:"p5m9q4", q:"A user table leaks. Roughly how long does testing all 26^8 eight-letter lowercase passwords take against unsalted SHA-256 at 20 billion hashes per second, versus a memory-hard hash allowing about 1,000 guesses per second?", options:["About 2.4 minutes versus about 64 years","About 10 seconds versus about 6.6 years","About 17 minutes versus about 660 years","About 10 seconds versus about 2.4 days"], answer:1, why:"26^8 is about 2.09 x 10^11. Divided by 2 x 10^10 per second that is about 10 seconds; divided by 1,000 per second it is about 2.09 x 10^8 seconds, roughly 6.6 years. The 2.4-minute and 64-year figures belong to the larger 36^8 space."},
 {id:"p5m9q5", q:"An API receives JWTs from a mobile app. Which verification approach is correct?", options:["Verify the signature using the algorithm named in the token's own header, then trust the claims","Decode the payload and trust it, since a JWT is encrypted and only the server can read it","Verify the signature with an algorithm fixed in server configuration, then check exp, iss and aud","Check exp, iss and aud only; the signature matters only when the token carries secrets"], answer:2, why:"The server chooses the algorithm in advance so a token cannot pick a weaker one, and a valid signature still needs unexpired, correctly issued claims meant for this audience. A JWT is signed, not encrypted: anyone holding it can read the payload, so its contents prove nothing unverified."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p6m1":[
 {id:"p6m1q1", q:"od -A x -t x1 -N 8 Report.class prints ca fe ba be 00 00 00 3d. Which Java release compiled it, and where will it load?", options:["Java 17; it loads on Java 21 and 25 but not on Java 11","Java 17; it loads on any JVM, because bytecode is portable across versions","Java 21; it loads only on Java 21 or newer","Java 13; it loads on Java 11 or newer"], answer:0, why:"The last two bytes are the major version: hex 3d is 61, and 61 - 44 = 17. A class file runs on its own release or newer, so Java 11 (major 55) rejects it with UnsupportedClassVersionError. Portability is across platforms, not backwards across Java versions."},
 {id:"p6m1q2", q:"Which javap -c listing matches static int f(int a, int b) { return a * b + a; }?", options:["iload_0, iload_1, iadd, iload_0, imul, ireturn","lload_0, lload_1, lmul, lload_0, ladd, lreturn","iload_0, iload_1, imul, iload_0, iadd, ireturn","imul r0, r0, r1; iadd r0, r0, r0; ireturn r0"], answer:2, why:"The JVM is a stack machine: push a and b, imul pops both and pushes the product, push a again, iadd, then return the top of the stack. The i prefix marks int operations; the l version is for longs, and bytecode has no registers like the last option."},
 {id:"p6m1q3", q:"A desktop app compiles fine against reporting-lib.jar, starts normally and runs for hours, then throws NoClassDefFoundError: com/acme/report/Pdf the first time someone presses Export. What explains both the error and its timing?", options:["The JIT deoptimized the export code and discarded the compiled class","Classes load on first use, and Pdf's JAR is missing from the run-time class path","The garbage collector unloaded Pdf after it sat unused for hours","The bytecode verifier checks each class only after the JVM has warmed up"], answer:1, why:"Class loaders load a class the first time it is needed, so a missing dependency surfaces only when Export first touches Pdf. NoClassDefFoundError means it existed at compile time but is absent at run time, or its initialization failed; read the first 'Caused by'. Deoptimization never removes classes."},
 {id:"p6m1q4", q:"-XX:+PrintCompilation shows '112  41 %  4  Parser::scan @ 17 (88 bytes)' and later the same line ending in 'made not entrant'. What happened?", options:["scan was interpreted 41 times and then evicted from the code cache","C1 compiled scan using 4% of the CPU, and the method was later garbage-collected","scan threw an exception at bytecode 17 and fell back to the interpreter permanently","C2 compiled a loop in scan via on-stack replacement; that code was later deoptimized"], answer:3, why:"% marks an on-stack-replacement compile entered mid-loop at bytecode index 17, and 4 is the C2 tier; 41 is just the compile ID. 'made not entrant' means the compiled code was invalidated, typically deoptimization after a speculation proved wrong, and the method can recompile later."},
 {id:"p6m1q5", q:"Main.java declares package com.acme.app. You compile with javac -d out src/com/acme/app/Main.java, then cd out and run java Main, which prints 'Error: Could not find or load main class Main'. What works?", options:["From the project root, run java -cp out com.acme.app.Main","From out/com/acme/app, run java Main.class","Rename Main.java to main.java and recompile","Recompile with javac --release 21 and run java Main again"], answer:0, why:"A class in a package must be launched by its fully qualified name, from the class-path root that contains the com/acme/app folders. java Main.class is never valid, the file name must match the public class exactly, and --release fixes version mismatches, not class-path errors."}
],
"p6m2":[
 {id:"p6m2q1", q:"What does this print? System.out.println(-17 / 5 + \" \" + -17 % 5 + \" \" + Math.floorMod(-17, 5));", options:["-4 3 3","-3 -2 -2","-4 -2 3","-3 -2 3"], answer:3, why:"Java's integer division truncates toward zero, so -17 / 5 is -3 and the remainder keeps the dividend's sign, -2. Math.floorMod gives the floor-based result, 3. '-4 3' is Python's answer, which Java reproduces only with Math.floorDiv and floorMod."},
 {id:"p6m2q2", q:"What does this print? System.out.println((int) -2.7 + \" \" + (byte) 300 + \" \" + (char) ('a' + 2));", options:["-3 44 c","-2 44 c","-2 127 c","-2 44 99"], answer:1, why:"Casting a double to int truncates toward zero (-2, not -3). Narrowing 300 to a byte keeps the low 8 bits: 300 - 256 = 44, with no clamping at 127. 'a' + 2 is the int 99, and the cast back to char makes it 'c'."},
 {id:"p6m2q3", q:"Given short s = 10; which of these statements fails to compile?", options:["s += 5;","s++;","s = s + 5;","int t = s + 5;"], answer:2, why:"Arithmetic on short is promoted to int, so s + 5 is an int and assigning it back to a short is a possible lossy conversion. Compound assignment and ++ include a hidden cast, so they compile, and storing the int result in an int is fine."},
 {id:"p6m2q4", q:"A class has three overloads: show(long x), show(Integer x) and show(Object x). Which ones do show(5) and show(5.0) call?", options:["show(long), then show(Object)","show(Integer), then show(Object)","show(long), then show(long)","show(Object) for both"], answer:0, why:"Overload resolution tries widening before boxing: int widens to long, so show(5) picks show(long) over show(Integer). A double cannot narrow to long, so show(5.0) needs boxing to Double, which fits only Object. Boxing never turns a double into an Integer."},
 {id:"p6m2q5", q:"Map<String, Integer> stock = new HashMap<>(); ... int n = stock.get(\"SKU-9\"); runs when SKU-9 is not in the map. What happens?", options:["n is 0, the default value for int","A NullPointerException on that line, from unboxing null","A compile error: Integer cannot be converted to int","n is -1, the map's missing-key marker"], answer:1, why:"get returns null for a missing key, and assigning it to an int auto-unboxes by calling intValue() on null, which throws NullPointerException on a line with no visible method call. Locals have no default, and the code compiles because unboxing is automatic."}
],
"p6m3":[
 {id:"p6m3q1", q:"class Base { Base() { describe(); } void describe() {} } class Splice extends Base { private String label = \"S-17\"; private int loss = 15; @Override void describe() { System.out.println(label + \" \" + loss); } } What does new Splice(); print?", options:["S-17 15","Nothing, because constructors never call overridden methods","null 0","A compile error: a constructor cannot call an overridable method"], answer:2, why:"new runs Base's constructor before Splice's field initializers, and Base calls describe(), which dispatches to the override. At that moment the fields hold only zeroed defaults, null and 0. That is why constructors must not call overridable methods; javac accepts the code without complaint."},
 {id:"p6m3q2", q:"On 64-bit HotSpot with a 12-byte object header and 4-byte compressed references, how big is each object of class Splice { String id; double lossDb; int tray; }, and roughly how much heap do a million of them take, not counting the Strings?", options:["32 bytes; about 32 MB","28 bytes; about 28 MB","16 bytes; about 16 MB","40 bytes; about 40 MB"], answer:0, why:"12 (header) + 4 (reference) + 8 (double) + 4 (int) = 28 bytes, padded to the next multiple of 8, so 32 bytes and about 32 MB per million. 28 forgets the padding and 16 counts only the fields; each String is a separate object."},
 {id:"p6m3q3", q:"final class Route { private final List<String> stops; Route(List<String> stops) { this.stops = stops; } List<String> stops() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(stops); } } Then: var list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"A\", \"B\")); var r = new Route(list); list.add(\"X\"); System.out.println(r.stops()); prints?", options:["[A, B]","UnsupportedOperationException","A compile error, because stops is final","[A, B, X]"], answer:3, why:"The constructor stored the caller's list itself, so the caller still holds a handle to Route's state; final stops reassignment of the field, not changes to the list. The read-only view protects only callers of stops(). Copy on the way in with List.copyOf(stops)."},
 {id:"p6m3q4", q:"Which version of the Money record compiles and rejects negative amounts?", options:["record Money(long cents) { Money { if (cents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); this.cents = cents; } }","record Money(long cents) { Money { if (cents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); } }","record Money(long cents) { Money(long cents) { if (cents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); } }","record Money(long cents) { private final long dollars = 0; Money { if (cents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); } }"], answer:1, why:"A compact constructor validates the parameters, and the field is assigned automatically afterwards. Assigning this.cents inside it is a compile error, a full canonical constructor must assign the field itself, and a record cannot declare extra instance fields."},
 {id:"p6m3q5", q:"class Job { static int count = 0; int id; Job() { count++; id = count; } } After Job a = new Job(), b = new Job(), c = new Job(); what does System.out.println(a.id + \" \" + c.id + \" \" + a.count) print?", options:["1 1 1","3 3 3","1 3 1","1 3 3"], answer:3, why:"count is static, so all three objects share one copy, which ends at 3; id is an instance field each object sets once, so a keeps 1 and c gets 3. a.count compiles, but it reads the class's single field, not a per-object value."}
],
"p6m4":[
 {id:"p6m4q1", q:"class A { String name = \"A\"; String who() { return \"A\"; } static String s() { return \"A\"; } } class B extends A { String name = \"B\"; @Override String who() { return \"B\"; } static String s() { return \"B\"; } } With A x = new B(); what does System.out.println(x.name + x.who() + x.s()) print?", options:["BBB","AAA","BBA","ABA"], answer:3, why:"Only instance methods are dispatched on the object's run-time class, so who() gives B. Fields are resolved by the reference's static type, A, and a static method with the same signature merely hides the parent's, so x.s() also uses A. BBB assumes everything is polymorphic."},
 {id:"p6m4q2", q:"class Printer { void print(Object o) { System.out.print(\"obj \"); } void print(String s) { System.out.print(\"str \"); } } With Printer p = new Printer(); Object o = \"hi\"; what do p.print(o); p.print(\"hi\"); print?", options:["obj str","str str","obj obj","str obj"], answer:0, why:"Overloads are chosen at compile time from the static type of the argument: o is declared Object, so print(Object) runs even though it holds a String. The literal's static type is String, so the second call picks print(String). The run-time class chooses overrides, not overloads."},
 {id:"p6m4q3", q:"final class Tag { final String id; Tag(String id) { this.id = id; } public boolean equals(Tag t) { return t.id.equals(id); } public int hashCode() { return id.hashCode(); } } A HashSet holds new Tag(\"x\"). What do set.contains(new Tag(\"x\")) and new Tag(\"x\").equals(new Tag(\"x\")) return?", options:["true and true","true and false","false and true","false and false"], answer:2, why:"equals(Tag) is an overload, not an override, so HashSet, which calls equals(Object), still gets Object's identity test and answers false even though the hash codes match. The direct call with a Tag argument picks the overload and returns true. @Override would have caught it."},
 {id:"p6m4q4", q:"interface Walker { default String move() { return \"walk\"; } } interface Swimmer { default String move() { return \"swim\"; } } class Animal { public String move() { return \"animal\"; } } class Duck extends Animal implements Walker, Swimmer {} What does new Duck().move() return?", options:["\"walk\", from the first interface listed","\"animal\", because a superclass method beats interface defaults","\"swim\", because the last interface listed takes priority","A compile error: Duck must override move() to choose"], answer:1, why:"The rule is 'class wins': a method inherited from a superclass takes priority over any interface default, which settles the conflict. Without Animal, Duck would fail to compile until it overrode move(), for example by returning Walker.super.move()."},
 {id:"p6m4q5", q:"Shape is sealed, permitting the records Circle, Rect and Tri. What happens with: return switch (s) { case Rect r -> r.w() * r.h(); case Rect(double w, double h) when w == h -> w * w; case Circle c -> Math.PI * c.r() * c.r(); case Tri t -> 0.5 * t.b() * t.h(); };", options:["It compiles, and squares take the guarded case","It compiles, but the guarded case is never reached","It fails to compile: the guarded Rect case is dominated by case Rect r","It fails to compile: a switch over a sealed type still needs a default"], answer:2, why:"case Rect r already matches every Rect, so the guarded pattern after it could never run, and javac rejects it as dominated; put the guarded case first. No default is needed, because the permits list lets the compiler prove the three cases cover every Shape."}
],
"p6m5":[
 {id:"p6m5q1", q:"You call new ArrayList<>() and then add 100 elements. ArrayList allocates 10 slots on the first add and grows by 50% (old + old/2) whenever it is full. What is the final capacity, and how many times did it grow after that first allocation?", options:["109, after 6 growths","128, after 4 growths","160, after 4 growths","100, after 90 growths"], answer:0, why:"Capacities run 10, 15, 22, 33, 49, 73, 109: six growths, each copying the array once. 160 assumes doubling from 10 and 128 assumes doubling from 16, which is HashMap's rule, not ArrayList's. new ArrayList<>(100) would avoid every copy."},
 {id:"p6m5q2", q:"Which of these generic assignments and calls compiles without error?", options:["List<Number> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>();","List<? extends Number> nums = new ArrayList<Integer>(); nums.add(5);","List<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>(); sink.add(5);","List<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>(); Integer x = sink.get(0);"], answer:2, why:"A ? super Integer list is a consumer: Integers can safely go in, but reads come back only as Object, so the last option fails. Generics are invariant, so List<Number> cannot hold an ArrayList<Integer>, and a ? extends list is a producer you may read from but not add to (PECS)."},
 {id:"p6m5q3", q:"A class declares void save(List<String> names) {} and void save(List<Integer> ids) {}. What happens?", options:["It compiles, and calls are routed by the element type at run time","Compile error: name clash, because both methods have the same erasure","It compiles, but the List<Integer> version can never be called","It compiles, then throws ClassCastException on the first call"], answer:1, why:"Type erasure turns both parameters into plain List in the bytecode, so the two methods would have identical signatures and javac rejects them. Element types do not exist at run time, which is also why instanceof List<String> and new T() are illegal. Give the methods different names."},
 {id:"p6m5q4", q:"A dispatcher must always pull the most urgent of thousands of open repair tickets while new ones arrive constantly. Several tickets often share the same urgency level. Which collection fits?", options:["A PriorityQueue ordered by urgency","A TreeSet with a comparator on urgency only","An ArrayList re-sorted after every insert","A HashMap keyed by urgency level"], answer:0, why:"A binary heap gives O(log n) offer and poll with O(1) peek at the most urgent item, and it keeps duplicates. A TreeSet treats a comparator result of 0 as equality, so tickets sharing an urgency silently vanish; re-sorting a list costs O(n log n) per insert."},
 {id:"p6m5q5", q:"Single-threaded code runs for (Splice s : splices) if (s.lossDb() > 0.3) splices.remove(s); on an ArrayList and throws ConcurrentModificationException. What is the right fix?", options:["Wrap the list with Collections.synchronizedList","Make splices a LinkedList, which allows removal while iterating","Catch the exception and continue with the next element","Replace the loop with splices.removeIf(s -> s.lossDb() > 0.3)"], answer:3, why:"Iterators are fail-fast: removing through the list changes its modification count, so the for-each iterator throws even with one thread. removeIf, or Iterator.remove(), removes safely. Synchronization addresses threads, which are not the problem, and a LinkedList's iterator is just as fail-fast."}
],
"p6m6":[
 {id:"p6m6q1", q:"Res implements AutoCloseable and its close() prints 'close ' + name; B's close() also throws. What does this print? try (Res a = new Res(\"A\"); Res b = new Res(\"B\")) { throw new IllegalStateException(\"body\"); } catch (Exception e) { System.out.println(\"caught \" + e.getMessage() + \" suppressed \" + e.getSuppressed().length); }", options:["close A, close B, caught body suppressed 1","close B, close A, caught body suppressed 1","close B, close A, caught close failed suppressed 0","caught body suppressed 0, then close B, close A"], answer:1, why:"Resources close in reverse order of opening, B then A, before the catch block runs. B's close failure is attached to the body's exception as a suppressed exception instead of replacing it, so the original message survives. A hand-written finally would have let the close failure win."},
 {id:"p6m6q2", q:"What does System.out.println(f()) print, given static int f() { try { throw new RuntimeException(\"x\"); } finally { return 7; } }?", options:["7, and the RuntimeException is silently discarded","Nothing; the RuntimeException propagates out of f","A compile error, because return in finally is unreachable","7, and then the RuntimeException is rethrown"], answer:0, why:"A return inside finally completes the method normally and throws away the pending exception, so the failure disappears without a trace. That is why return and throw do not belong in finally; javac compiles it, warning only with -Xlint."},
 {id:"p6m6q3", q:"What happens with try { Files.readString(p); } catch (IOException e) { log(e); } catch (NoSuchFileException e) { createDefault(); }?", options:["It compiles, and a missing file runs createDefault() in the second block","It compiles, and both catch blocks run for a missing file","It compiles, but NoSuchFileException is unchecked, so neither block runs","It does not compile: the IOException block already catches NoSuchFileException"], answer:3, why:"NoSuchFileException is a subclass of IOException, so the first block already catches it and the second could never run; javac rejects the unreachable catch. Order catch blocks from specific to general. Only one catch block ever runs for a given exception."},
 {id:"p6m6q4", q:"static void reset(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append(\"!\"); sb = new StringBuilder(\"new\"); sb.append(\"?\"); } Then: StringBuilder s = new StringBuilder(\"old\"); reset(s); System.out.println(s); What prints?", options:["old","old!","new?","old!new?"], answer:1, why:"Java passes the reference by value: the append before reassignment mutates the caller's object, giving old!. Reassigning the parameter only changes the method's local copy of the reference, so the new builder and its ? never reach the caller."},
 {id:"p6m6q5", q:"An app server throws OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace after its 30th hot redeploy, while the GC log shows heap after-values flat around 200 MB of a 2 GB heap. What is the likely cause and first move?", options:["The heap is too small for the app; raise -Xmx to 4 GB","Objects are leaking on the heap; take a GC.class_histogram of byte[]","A class-loader leak from redeploys; check jcmd VM.metaspace","G1 cannot keep up with redeploys; switch to ZGC"], answer:2, why:"Class metadata lives in metaspace, native memory outside the heap, and old class loaders kept alive by a reference pin every class they loaded. A flat after-GC heap rules out a heap leak, so a bigger -Xmx or a different collector cannot help."}
],
"p6m7":[
 {id:"p6m7q1", q:"On Linux, java -cp out com.acme.app.Main fails with NoClassDefFoundError: com/acme/fiber/Otdr. That class lives in lib/fiber.jar, and Main.java already has import com.acme.fiber.Otdr;. Which command fixes it?", options:["java -cp out;lib/fiber.jar com.acme.app.Main","java -cp out com.acme.app.Main lib/fiber.jar","java -cp out:lib/fiber.jar com.acme.app.Main","java -cp out com.acme.app.Main, after moving the import to the top of the file"], answer:2, why:"The JVM looks for com/acme/fiber/Otdr.class in each class-path entry, so the JAR must be on it, joined with : on Linux (; is the Windows separator). A trailing argument just goes to main, and import is only a compile-time naming shortcut that loads nothing."},
 {id:"p6m7q2", q:"Your pom declares A 1.0, which depends on json-lib 2.5, and B 3.1, which depends on C 1.2, which depends on json-lib 2.9. At run time B throws NoSuchMethodError from json-lib. What happened, and what is the fix?", options:["Nearest wins picked 2.5; check dependency:tree and pin 2.9 in dependencyManagement","Maven always picks the newest version, so json-lib 2.9 itself is broken; downgrade B","Maven loaded both versions, and the JVM picked one at random; run mvn clean install","json-lib needs test scope so it is not shipped twice in the JAR"], answer:0, why:"Maven mediates conflicts by 'nearest wins': json-lib 2.5 sits two levels down, 2.9 three, so 2.5 is chosen silently and B calls a method it lacks. dependency:tree shows the mediation, and dependencyManagement or a BOM pins the version you need. Maven does not prefer the newest."},
 {id:"p6m7q3", q:"A new class LossBudgetChecks, with methods annotated org.junit.jupiter.api.Test, passes when run in the IDE, but mvn test never runs it and its tests are missing from the Tests run count. What is the most likely cause?", options:["JUnit must be declared with compile scope for Surefire to see it","Jupiter test classes must be public to be discovered by Maven","The class needs JUnit 4's @RunWith to be picked up by Maven","Surefire runs only *Test, Test*, *Tests or *TestCase classes; rename it"], answer:3, why:"The IDE runs whatever you click, but Surefire discovers test classes by name pattern, and 'Checks' matches none of them, so rename it LossBudgetTest. Test scope is correct for JUnit, Jupiter allows package-private classes, and @RunWith is the JUnit 4 mechanism."},
 {id:"p6m7q4", q:"Pick the Maven scopes, in order, for: (1) JUnit Jupiter; (2) a PostgreSQL JDBC driver your code never references directly but needs at run time; (3) the servlet API, which the Tomcat server supplies.", options:["test, runtime, provided","compile, compile, compile","test, provided, runtime","provided, runtime, test"], answer:0, why:"test keeps JUnit out of the shipped artifact; runtime puts the driver on the run-time class path without compiling against it; provided compiles against the servlet API but does not package it, because the server brings its own. Making everything compile ships unneeded and conflicting JARs."},
 {id:"p6m7q5", q:"module com.you.splice { exports com.you.splice; } contains the package com.you.splice.app, whose class Main is public. Another module that requires com.you.splice references com.you.splice.app.Main. What happens?", options:["It compiles, because Main is declared public","It fails to compile: com.you.splice.app is not exported","It compiles but fails at run time with NoClassDefFoundError","It compiles, because requires grants access to every package of the module"], answer:1, why:"The module system adds strong encapsulation above access modifiers: only exported packages are readable by other modules, so public alone is not enough. requires makes the exported API available, not the internals, and the error is caught at compile time rather than mid-request."}
],
"p6m8":[
 {id:"p6m8q1", q:"A service runs on 16 cores. Each request computes for 10 ms and waits 40 ms on a remote API. Using threads = cores x (1 + wait time / compute time), how many platform threads should its pool have?", options:["16","64","80","640"], answer:2, why:"16 x (1 + 40/10) = 16 x 5 = 80 threads keeps every core busy while the others wait. 16 is the rule for purely CPU-bound work, 64 forgets the 1 for the compute share, and 640 multiplies by the raw wait time."},
 {id:"p6m8q2", q:"You move a blocking service to Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor(). Its database allows only 50 connections, and at peak 5,000 requests run at once. How should you protect the database?", options:["Create a fixed pool of 50 virtual threads and submit every request to it","Bound access with a Semaphore of 50 permits or the 50-connection pool","Return to 50 platform threads for the whole service","Shrink the thread stack size with -Xss so fewer threads fit"], answer:1, why:"Virtual threads are cheap and should never be pooled; limit the scarce resource itself, so threads wait cheaply for a permit or connection. Pooling virtual threads or going back to 50 platform threads throttles all the work, including requests that never touch the database."},
 {id:"p6m8q3", q:"Several threads count words into a shared HashMap<String, Integer> with: if (!counts.containsKey(w)) counts.put(w, 1); else counts.put(w, counts.get(w) + 1); Totals come out slightly low. Which fix is correct?", options:["Declare the counts field volatile so every thread sees updates","Wrap it in Collections.synchronizedMap and keep the same code","Switch to ConcurrentHashMap and keep the same containsKey/put code","Use ConcurrentHashMap.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum) in place of both branches"], answer:3, why:"The bug is a check-then-act plus a read-modify-write: another thread can act between the calls. merge on a ConcurrentHashMap does the whole update atomically. A synchronized or concurrent map makes each single call safe but not the sequence, and volatile only affects the reference."},
 {id:"p6m8q4", q:"transfer(from, to) locks from, then to. transfer(A, B) and transfer(B, A) run at the same moment; the process hangs at 0% CPU and jcmd <pid> Thread.print reports 'Found one Java-level deadlock'. What is the fix?", options:["Always lock the two accounts in one global order, such as lower account id first","Declare the account balance fields volatile so both threads see them","Enlarge the thread pool so another thread can finish the transfer","Run each transfer on its own virtual thread so neither blocks a carrier"], answer:0, why:"Each thread holds one lock and waits for the other's: a circular wait. Acquiring locks in a single global order removes the cycle (tryLock with a timeout is the alternative). volatile adds visibility, not exclusion, and more or cheaper threads still wait on the same two monitors."},
 {id:"p6m8q5", q:"Thread A runs config = new Config(url, timeout); where Config's fields are not final and the static config field is a plain field. Thread B sometimes sees a non-null config whose timeout is still 0. Which change guarantees B sees the fully built object?", options:["Have thread B sleep 100 ms before reading config, so A can finish","Check config != null twice, as in double-checked locking, before using it","Declare the static config field volatile, or make Config's fields final","Run thread B as a virtual thread on the same carrier as A"], answer:2, why:"Without a happens-before edge the write of the reference can become visible before the constructor's field writes. A volatile write and read create that edge, and final fields are guaranteed visible once the object is seen. Sleeping or rechecking only changes timing, which proves nothing under the memory model."}
],
"p6m9":[
 {id:"p6m9q1", q:"What does this print? List<Integer> xs = List.of(4, 9, 2, 7, 11); Optional<Integer> r = xs.stream().peek(x -> System.out.print(x + \" \")).filter(x -> x > 5).findFirst(); System.out.println(\"-> \" + r.get());", options:["4 9 2 7 11 -> 9","4 9 -> 9","9 -> 9","-> 9"], answer:1, why:"Streams are lazy and process one element at a time through every stage. 4 is peeked and filtered out, 9 is peeked and passes, and findFirst short-circuits, so 2, 7 and 11 are never pulled. Printing all five assumes each stage finishes the whole list first."},
 {id:"p6m9q2", q:"record Job(String crew, int hours) {} and jobs = List.of(new Job(\"A\", 3), new Job(\"B\", 5), new Job(\"A\", 4), new Job(\"C\", 2), new Job(\"B\", 1)). What does jobs.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(Job::crew, Job::hours)) produce?", options:["{A=4, B=1, C=2}, because the last value wins","{A=3, B=5, C=2}, because the first value wins","A compile error, because the keys may repeat","An IllegalStateException for the duplicate key A"], answer:3, why:"toMap without a merge function refuses duplicate keys and throws at run time when the second A arrives. Passing a merge function, toMap(Job::crew, Job::hours, Integer::sum), or using groupingBy with summingInt gives {A=7, B=6, C=2}. Unlike HashMap.put, toMap never silently overwrites."},
 {id:"p6m9q3", q:"int threshold = 10; if (strict) threshold = 5; list.removeIf(x -> x < threshold); What happens?", options:["A compile error: threshold is not effectively final, since it is reassigned","It compiles, and the lambda reads threshold's latest value when removeIf runs","It compiles, but removeIf throws ConcurrentModificationException","A compile error: removeIf needs a Function, not a Predicate"], answer:0, why:"A lambda captures the value of a local variable, so the variable must be final or effectively final, never reassigned. Computing the value once into a new final local fixes it. removeIf takes a Predicate, which this lambda fits, and it removes safely without ConcurrentModificationException."},
 {id:"p6m9q4", q:"List<Integer> results = new ArrayList<>(); IntStream.range(0, 100_000).parallel().filter(i -> i % 3 == 0).forEach(results::add); The size of results varies between runs, and now and then an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException appears. What is the right fix?", options:["Declare results as a volatile field so all threads see each add","Switch results to a LinkedList, which has no array to overflow","Call .unordered() before forEach so the threads stop colliding","Let the stream build the list itself with .boxed().toList()"], answer:3, why:"forEach on a parallel stream runs on several ForkJoinPool threads, and ArrayList.add is a non-atomic read-modify-write on shared state, so updates collide. Letting the stream collect its own result is correct and thread-safe. volatile covers only the reference, and LinkedList is just as unsafe."},
 {id:"p6m9q5", q:"The lesson's SpliceService receives curl -i -X POST -d 'cable=C3&fiber=2' http://127.0.0.1:8091/splices, with no lossDb field. What does it return?", options:["201 Created, storing lossDb as 0.00 by default","500 Internal Server Error with a Java stack trace","400 Bad Request with {\"error\":\"NullPointerException\"}","405 Method Not Allowed, since the body is incomplete"], answer:2, why:"f.get(\"lossDb\") returns null, and Double.parseDouble(null) throws NullPointerException; the handler catches any RuntimeException and answers 400 with the exception's simple name. Validation at the boundary returns 4xx instead of leaking a stack trace, and 405 is only for methods other than GET and POST."}
]
});
